{"name":"Donkey Kong","hex":"#ab4210","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Donkey Kong","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An uncivilized or ill-mannered person; a boor.","A penis."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Donkey_Kong"}],"entries":[{"title":"Donkey Kong","blocks":[{"text":"Donkey Kong is a video game series and media franchise created by the Japanese game designer Shigeru Miyamoto for Nintendo. It follows the adventures of Donkey Kong, a large, powerful gorilla, and other members of the Kong family of simians. Donkey Kong games include the original arcade game trilogy by Nintendo R&D1; the Donkey Kong Country series by Rare and Retro Studios; and the Mario vs. Donkey Kong series by Nintendo Software Technology. Various studios have developed spin-offs in genres such as edutainment, puzzle, racing, and rhythm. The franchise also incorporates animation, printed media, theme parks, and merchandise."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donkey_Kong","wikidata":"Q662004"},{"title":"Donkey Kong (1981 video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Donkey Kong is a 1981 platform game developed and published by Nintendo for arcades. As Mario, the player runs and jumps onto platforms and climbs ladders to ascend a construction site and rescue Pauline from the giant gorilla Donkey Kong. It is the first game in the Donkey Kong series and Mario's first appearance in a video game."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/14/Donkey_Kong_flier.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donkey_Kong_(1981_video_game)","wikidata":"Q12384"},{"title":"Donkey","blocks":[{"text":"The donkey or ass is a domesticated equine. It derives from the African wild ass, Equus africanus, and may be classified either as a subspecies thereof, Equus africanus asinus, or as a separate species, Equus asinus. It was domesticated in Africa some 5000–7000 years ago, and has been used mainly as a working animal since that time."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Donkey_in_Clovelly%2C_North_Devon%2C_England.jpg/330px-Donkey_in_Clovelly%2C_North_Devon%2C_England.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Donkey","wikidata":"Q19707"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Shigeru_Miyamoto_20150610_%28cropped_2%29.jpg/500px-Shigeru_Miyamoto_20150610_%28cropped_2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Shigeru_Miyamoto_20150610_%28cropped_2%29.jpg/500px-Shigeru_Miyamoto_20150610_%28cropped_2%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Donkey Kong creator Shigeru Miyamoto in 2015","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AShigeru_Miyamoto_20150610_(cropped_2).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Tim_and_Chris_Stamper_outside_the_FortuneFish_offices.jpg/500px-Tim_and_Chris_Stamper_outside_the_FortuneFish_offices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Tim_and_Chris_Stamper_outside_the_FortuneFish_offices.jpg/1280px-Tim_and_Chris_Stamper_outside_the_FortuneFish_offices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Tim_and_Chris_Stamper_outside_the_FortuneFish_offices.jpg/500px-Tim_and_Chris_Stamper_outside_the_FortuneFish_offices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rare founders Tim and Chris Stamper (pictured in 2015) led the development of Donkey Kong Country (1994), which reestablished Donkey Kong as a major franchise.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATim_and_Chris_Stamper_ou